WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.460
Your first batch of liquid soap, tested and scaled.

2
00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:08.760
This is the video for module two of Detergent and Cleaning Products Production.

3
00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:15.560
Put on goggles, gloves and sleeves, with rinse water ready, and start from a formula you trust.

4
00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:26.390
First, sprinkle nitrosol slowly onto the measured water while stirring. Dumped in, it forms lumps that never dissolve. Let it swell until clear and thick.

5
00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:35.460
In separate plastic containers, add caustic soda slowly to cold water and let it cool. Dissolve soda ash in another.

6
00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:43.070
Pour the sulphonic acid slowly into the thickened water, stirring in one direction. Keep your face back.

7
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:54.180
Add the alkali solutions in small portions. Soda ash will fizz and the batch will warm. After each portion, test a sample with a pH strip.

8
00:00:55.430 --> 00:01:04.090
Stir in the SLES. Adjust thickness. When the batch has cooled, add colour, perfume and preservative at the doses on their data sheets.

9
00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:15.390
Cover and rest it for 12 to 24 hours so foam settles. Check the pH again, then bottle in clean containers, with a batch number on every one.

10
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:27.690
Acid strength and caustic purity vary between suppliers, so a fixed amount of caustic can be wrong. Below about pH 6, acid remains and the soap will sting hands.

11
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:32.710
Well above 9 means too much alkali, which is harsh on skin.

12
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:47.890
For example, Ngozi in Enugu makes a 20-litre batch with acid from a new supplier. After adding the amounts on her sheet, the strip shows about pH 5. She stops. She does not go on to the SLES stage.

13
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:55.990
She dissolves a little extra soda ash and adds it in two small portions, stirring and testing after each.

14
00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:05.950
The strip now shows about pH 7. She writes the extra amount and the supplier's name in her batch record, so she knows for next time.

15
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:20.980
A full 25-litre batch on your first try is how money gets wasted. Test small first. For example, Halima's trainer gave her a 20-litre formula. One line says 100 ml of perfume.

16
00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:26.050
She divides by 20 to get the amount per litre: 5 ml.

17
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.840
For a 2-litre test batch, 5 times 2 is 10 ml.

18
00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:45.230
For a 25-litre batch, 5 times 25 is 125 ml. She does this for every line, so the test batch truly predicts the big one. And she changes only one thing at a time.

19
00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:53.580
Salt thickens SLES products. Dissolve it in water and add a small portion at a time. Stir, wait and check.

20
00:02:53.930 --> 00:03:01.550
Time how many seconds the product takes to run out of the same small cup with a hole. Now thick becomes a number you can repeat.

21
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:12.510
But salt only works up to a peak. Keep adding and the liquid gets thinner again, and you cannot take the salt out. If it gets thinner, stop and note the amount.

22
00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:22.310
A test batch is ready to scale when it passes every check two times in a row. pH near neutral. The flow time you want. No lumps.

23
00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:30.020
It removes palm oil from a plate well, and it does not separate after a few days. Then it becomes your master formula.

24
00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:43.770
Customers will ask for something that kills germs. Your liquid soap is a cleaner, not a disinfectant. Teach them to use household bleach. First, clean the surface with detergent and water.

25
00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:59.610
For bleach of 5% to 9% strength, the US CDC guidance works out to roughly 20 ml per litre of water, left on for at least 1 minute. Brands differ, so read the label and follow its directions.

26
00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:08.460
Make it fresh every day, in a labelled container, never a drink bottle. And never mix bleach with your soap or any other cleaner.

27
00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:20.250
So remember. Neutralise in portions and check pH each time. Write formulas per litre. Change one thing at a time. Salt thickens only up to a peak.

28
00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:27.890
Your hand wash will be mostly SLES, near neutral and always preserved. And never claim your detergent kills germs.

29
00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:39.360
Your practice: write your production card, make test batches A and B with one change, make a 1-litre hand wash test batch, and write a bleach card for customers.

30
00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:41.870
Now continue with the lessons below.
